/*****************************************************************************
    simple -- respelled to today's spellings (2026-11-11, SEQ 621) as the first form that draws rounded.
    Define-only; read and drawn by incant/pop/roundedT. See the notes below bail().
*****************************************************************************/
register(FORMs);
define
    simple window x=300 y=450 width=400 height=200 down rounded font:=normalFont
        third across fill:=wall
            wrap fill:=white
                one="This is a whole lot of text that I am using to test out text views, scrolling, and isn't it nice that the xcode text editor wraps lines and indents auto-magically. The base rule is you have to specify everything you want, nothing implied except styles are inherited." text;
            two fill:=red leftBorder=2 width=40;
            three fill:=white leftBorder=2 width=160;
        fourth rounded fill:=lightGreen height=60;
    ;
bail();

The old form, respelled by the forms rulings (Topics/forms). What changed, and why:
    the preamble       Start, the includes, initFORMs and printDefinition are gone: a form is define-only (initFORMs stops a run silently, F-73)
    down               added: third and fourth stack, as they did
    fill=, font        respelled fill:= and font:= (an attribute naming a field is :=)
    pad, border=off    dropped by ruling; border is not inherited, so border=off is deleted
    left=2             respelled leftBorder=2
    register           dropped (the drop list)
    width=50.0%        percent has no ruling (Waiting on Tony 4); three is 160 points and two is 40 until it does
    stuff text=one     one field showing another's text is the listener's family, parked; three is empty
    onLayout code={}   dropped by ruling; nothing fires
    height=60          added to fourth, so third keeps most of the form
    rounded            kept on simple and on fourth. simple names no fill and no border, so its rounded draws nothing; fourth draws at the default radius, 5.
